1. e4 { Because e4 is the most common opening move for white, when black persists in responding with the Dutch, it will lead to the Duras gambit } 1... f5 { White has a decisive advantage but should beware of Black playing fxe4 } { [%cal Rf5e4] } 2. exf5 { exf5 is beneficial because it threatens to play Qh5+ and prevents taking the white pawn on e4 (fxe4). Besides that it discourage playing e6 or e5 by guarding square e6 and forces Nf6. It intends to play d4. } { [%cal Re7e6,Re7e5,Gd1h5,Gf5e6,Gg8f6,Gf5g6,Bd2d4] } 2... Nf6 { This is the only good move although Magnus Carlsen played Kf7 once. } { [%cal Re8f7] } (2... Kf7 { This is the move Magnus Carlsen played in one of his online games during the Lichess "Titled Arena May '19" on 04.05.2019. He managed to win although this is a bad move. }) 3. d4 { d4 is good because it guards e5 and allows playing Bd3. But watch for d5 which allows the bishop to attack the white pawn at f5 (Bxf5) by making way through d7. } { [%cal Gd4e5,Gf1d3,Gd3f5,Bd7d5,Bc8f5] } 3... e6 { fxe6 is good because it prevents taking the white pawn on f5 (exf5)
and captures the black pawn and deflects the black pawn away from d7 when it recaptures and rules out playing d5 and creates an isolated pion. } { [%cal Gf5e6,Bd7e6,Rd7d5,Re6f5] } 4. Nf3 { Nf3 is good because it vacates g1 and enables O-O later on. Furtermore it enables Ng5. Maybe white intends to play fxe6 as the following move but black can take of course first. Most players will normally have taken fxe6. } { [%cal Ge6f5] } (4. fxe6 { fxe6 is good because it prevents taking the white pawn on f5 (exf5)
and captures the black pawn and deflects the black pawn away from d7 when it recaptures and rules out playing d5 and creates an isolated pion. } { [%cal Rd7e6] }) 4... exf5 { exf5 gets back the pawn and prevents white from taking the black pawn on e6 (fxe6). } 5. Bd3 { White has two intentions with this move. It intends to play O-O
and threatens the black pawn on f5. But black has Nc6 to guard e5
and can Bb4+ } { [%cal Bf8b4,Gf6e4,Rd3f5,Re1g1,Rh1f1] } 5... d6 { d6 is fine because it supports the f5-pawn through the bishop on Bc8 and prevents the knight to attack in the center. } { [%cal Gc8f5,Rd3f5,Rf3e5] } 6. O-O Be7 { Be7 is beneficial because it supports playing O-O. This could be challenged by Ng5 and Bc4. When that happens black however can defend that easily. } { [%cal Rf3g5,Rd3c4,Bd6d5,Bh7h6] } 7. c4 Na6 { Na6 helps Black develop the queenside knight, which was still on its starting square. Moving this pieceprepares for possible future moves like c5 or Nb4, attacking the White's d3 bishop.
Flexibility: Na6 is a flexible move that does not commit Black to any particular pawn structure or plan. It keeps the option to play f5-f4 later, or to support the pawn push d6-d5 in the center.
Control of key squares: The knight on a6 can support a pawn advance to c5, contesting the central d4 square and potentially opening lines for Black's pieces. } { [%cal Ga6b4,Gc7c5,Ga6c5,Gd6c5] } 8. a3 O-O 9. Nc3 c6 10. Re1 Nc7 *


